I need to get a copy of my military discharge papers (DD-214) and then use it to apply for VA benefits. I don't have the original and I'm not sure which agency holds the record. What are the exact steps, forms, and agencies involved — from requesting the record to using it for a VA claim? Please cite the current official sources.


Who holds the record

The National Archives (usually the National Personnel Records Center (NPRC) in St. Louis) is the main place to get a DD Form 214 (Report of Separation) and related Official Military Personnel File (OMPF) documents. Copies for veterans and next of kin are generally free. Some later records stay with the service branch; SF-180 page 3 and eVetRecs tell you the correct address.

Official pages: Request military service records (NARA) · VA: get military service records · About OMPFs / DD-214

If you are applying for VA benefits, VA says it will request your DD-214 when it receives your application. You do not have to order it from NARA first. Source: va.gov/records/get-military-service-records.

Request a DD-214 yourself

Online (eVetRecs)

• Open NARA eVetRecs (also linked from VA).
• Choose Make a new request.
• Sign in with a verified ID.me account (NARA may ask for a live selfie).
• Ask for an undeleted DD-214 if you need it for benefits (character of service and related codes).

Mail or fax — form SF-180

• Download Standard Form 180, Request Pertaining to Military Records: archives.gov SF-180.
• Fill in as much as you can: full name used in service, service number and/or SSN, branch, dates of service, date/place of birth. For 1973 NPRC fire cases, also last unit, place of entry, place of discharge.
• Sign and date the request (veteran or next of kin). Email is not accepted for the actual records request (Privacy Act / written request).
• Send to the address on page 3 of the SF-180 (most often NPRC). Typical NPRC contact from NARA:
• Mail: National Personnel Records Center, Military Personnel Records, 1 Archives Drive, St. Louis, MO 63138
• Fax: 314-801-9195
• Phone (status/general): 314-801-0800
• Do not send originals. Do not send a second request before 90 days.

Status after about 10 days: NARA’s online status update (existing requests only).

1973 fire: Some Army/Air Force records were destroyed. NPRC can often reconstruct enough for a VA claim if you give extra identifying details. VA also has a note on reconstructing records for disability claims: va.gov records page.

NARA warns that private “DD-214 research” companies charge for a service NARA provides for free.

Use the record for VA benefits

VA reviews discharge papers (DD-214 or equivalent) and service treatment records. You can still apply if you do not have the original.

Disability compensation

• Check eligibility: How to file a VA disability claim
• Form: VA Form 21-526EZ (Application for Disability Compensation and Related Compensation Benefits)
• Ways to file: online at that page; mail the form; in person at a VA regional office; or with an accredited VSO/attorney/claims agent
• Optional: VA Form 21-0966 Intent to File if you will file on paper and want to protect an effective date
• Evidence you may send: DD-214 if you have it, service treatment records, private medical records. VA can help gather federal records.

VA health care

• Form: VA Form 10-10EZ — How to apply for VA health care
• VA enrollment can still help if you cannot locate a DD-214.

Track claims: va.gov/claim-or-appeal-status · VA benefits line: 800-827-1000

I cannot verify every branch-specific cutoff date on SF-180 page 3 from the snippets above; use the current form and eVetRecs for the exact holding agency for your service dates.

America plans to let you file a VA disability claim right here in 2027. See what's coming.

