Empirical Comparison: DD-214 & VA Claim Retrieval
The second response from unauthenticated GPT reinforces the exact same conclusion established in the first test.
Feature / Dimension	General GPT (No-Login Baseline)	Specialized Domain Expectation (Murk)
Exact Form Numbers	SF-180 (Military Records), VA Form 21-526EZ (Disability Compensation), VA Form 21-0966 (Intent to File)	Identifies standard forms (SF-180, 21-526EZ).
Branch & Date Routing	Explicitly distinguishes milConnect dates by branch (Air Force >2004, Army >2002, USMC >1999, Navy >1995), National Guard (Adjutant General), and Reserve custodians.	Distinguishes NPRC vs. State Adjutant General vs. active branch records.
1973 NPRC Fire Mechanics	Identifies branch/date parameters (Army 1912–1960, Air Force 1947–1964) and explains the alternate records reconstruction process.	Highlights the fire boundaries and notes that VA/NARA use auxiliary sources to reconstruct service.
Procedural Edge Cases	Points out that VA requests the DD-214 on the veteran's behalf if filing a claim, meaning missing records do not block claim submission.	Outlines the sequence between requesting records and filing a claim.
Core Conclusions from Both Tests
Retrieval Depth is Model-Level, Not System-Level:
When prompted, base frontier models possess the parametric knowledge and web-retrieval capability to identify granular agency forms, specific branch/date routing criteria, and administrative exceptions. Specialized models like Murk do not hold a unique capability advantage over general-purpose models in finding this information.
The Role of the Specialized Wrapper:
What specialized domain wrappers (like Murk) provide is scoping and operational guardrails:
Bounded Domain Focus: Preventing the output from expanding into unnecessary edge cases unless specifically asked.
Citation Rules: Forcing strict .gov source attribution and verified, clickable hyperlinks on every claim.
Structured Guardrails: Ensuring the model stays consistently inside official federal lanes.
The empirical evidence from both queries confirms that specialized government AI tools excel at reliability, boundary enforcement, and auditability, while general models match or exceed them in raw retrieval scope when prompted broadly.

