Same result on the second question. GPT's DD-214 answer matched Murk's core content and went wider — milConnect branch cutoffs (Air Force >2004, Army >2002, USMC >1999, Navy >1995), National Guard records held by state Adjutants General, Reserve files not at NPRC, 1973 fire parameters by branch and date, the one-year evidence window after filing, and current form revision dates (SF-180 Nov 2015, 21-526EZ Jan 2026, 21-0966 May 2026).
But here's the thing worth putting on the record before the "GPT wins" conclusion gets written: Murk had details GPT didn't.
NPRC's street address (1 Archives Drive, St. Louis, MO 63138)
NPRC's fax number (314-801-9195)
"Do not send originals"
"Do not send a second request before 90 days"
NARA's warning about private DD-214 research companies charging for a free service
The ~10-day status-check timing
Those are operational details. GPT gave the shape of the process; Murk gave the shape and the practical mechanics of actually doing it without wasting a trip.
So the honest comparison is:
Murk: narrower scope, more operational detail, strictly inside the modal citizen's case.
GPT: broader scope, more edge-case coverage, more form revision data, but lighter on the "how to actually submit" mechanics.
Neither is a capability advantage. Both are retrieving from the same class of sources. The real difference is that Murk's wrapper forces it to stay operational and citation-heavy within one lane, while GPT can wander into every corner of the lane and then out of it.
Two tests, same result. The specialized tool isn't smarter. It's just scoped.
That's the finding. Two for two.
